1) Network:
LAN(Local Area Network) - Intranet - Private
WAN(Wide Area Network) - Internet + ISP - Public
2) Network Architecture:
* Client-Server Model
* Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Model
3) Tunneling or Encapsulation Evasion:
* Each layer accepts the payload from the layer above it blindly.
4) PDU(Protocol Data Units):
* Application layer - data
* Transport layer - segments or datagrams
* Internet layer - packets
* Network Access layer - Frames and bits
5) MAC Address:
* 48 bit colon separated value - 24 bit OUI and 24 bit NIC specific
OUI(Organizationally Unique Identifier) - identify manufacturer
NIC(Network Interface Card) - Unique serial number
6) ARP protocol - Layer 2:(Address Resolution Protocol)
* bridges gap between layer 3 IP and layer two MAC addresses.
* ARP Request - Broadcast, ARP Reply - Unicast, ARP Cache - Stores data
Exploit:
ARP Poisoning or ARP Spoofing - MITM attack - Sending fake ARP Reply to the victim.
Prevention:
To defend ARP Spoofing - use DAI(Dynamic ARP Inspector) on switch to prevent MITM(Man-In-The-Middle) - matches MAC address from the port to which ARP Request is send.

7) IPv4 - 32 bit doted decimals - Eg: 192.168.1.10 - each section can contain 8 bit of 1s or 0s.
* Each part can be from 0 to 255
* Network Portion - part that describes specific network
* Host Portion - part that describes the specific host device
8) Subnetting:(Divide and Conquer)
* The process of dividing large network into manageable and secure subnets.
* Subnetting allows segmentation which makes the attacker within a network "blast radius"
* Subnet Mask - acts as a filter - Eg: 255.255.255.0 where 255 represents Network Portion and 0 represents
Host Portion
* For a device to talk to other device within the same network does not require router while communicating external network requires the router's default gateway
* CIDR(Classless Inter-Domain Routing) - shorthand of counts of 1s in a subnet's network portion - 
Eg: 192.168.1.0/24 means the network part is composed of 24 1s and remaining 8 bits can be used for host part.
9) Public IP: 
* globally unique - routable on Open internet - leased from ISP or cloud provider.
10) Private IP: 
* Reserved for internal use - not routable on public internet - cannot access public website directly.
* 10.0.0.0 to 10.255.255.255 - Massive enterprise networks
* 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255 - Medium sized networks
* 192.168.0.0 to 192.168.255.255 - Home routers and small offices
11) NAT(Network Address Translation):
* router has public IP and end user has private IP
* when end user want to access public internet it uses private IP by router

12) IPv6 - 64 bit hexadecimal separated by colons
Difference: 
* No Broadcast < Multicast and Anycast  
* Simplified Header - fast routing process
* SLAAC(Stateless Address Autoconfiguration) - devices itself generate IPv6 based on router's prefix and MAC address.
Exploit: 
* IPv6 Tunneling - Firewall haven't configured to inspect IPv6 - encapsulate IPv4 inside IPv6
13) ICMP(Internet Control Message Protocol):
* caries control, diagnostic and error messages
* Destination unreachable(Type 3) - when not available in routing table or blocked by local firewall
* Time Exceeded(Type 11) - IP packets whose TTL(Time to Live) reduces by 1 for every hop(when reaches next router) when it reaches 0 it sends this ICMP message.
14) Ping - measure RTT(Round Trip Time) - gives latency of the network
* uses Echo request(Type 8) and Echo Reply(Type 0) 
* Request Timed out - server is offline or firewall hide server existence when pinged

15) Traceroute - maps all router till the target server
* abuses TTL and ICMP Time exceeded error and is not blocked by firewall as ICMP is used for diagnostics.
* First send packet with TTL of 1 then the ICMP is created and send back to client then the IP of the router is mapped and sends packet incremented TTL - Finally when destination reached sends ICMP Port Unreachable 
Weaponizing ICMP:
* ICMP Reconnaissance - Performing "ping sweep" which sends echo requests to every IPs in subnets and narrows down target by reply.
* ICMP Tunneling - In compromised machine "ptunnel" to encapsulate malicious code within ICMP echo request which received by external server and extracts the code and returns output in ICMP echo reply.
* ICMP Flood - Overwhelm a target by sending a massive ICMP echo requests and waste the target system's CPU and bandwidth to generate echo reply freezing the system. 
16) Ports - 16 bit numbers from 0 to 65535 - used for routing data internally.
* Port Multiplexing - single IP can make multiple conversation
* 0 - 1023 Standard system services, 1024 - 49151 Specific applications, 49152 - 65535 Ephemeral ports
17) TCP/IP - 3-Way Handshake(SYN,SYN-ACK,ACK) - Sequencing, Acknowledgements and Retransmissions
* "Stateful Firewalls" - keeps track of ACK that is it sent from the same SYN requested device
18) UDP - no handshakes, no acknowledgements and no sequencing
* unreliable but fast than TCP/IP
Exploit:
* UDP Reflection/Amplification Attack - flooding UDP requests to take down websites via DDOS(Distributed Denial of Service).
